#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

# What traged-deploy.service actually runs: pull the current TrageD server image
# and restart only that service.
#
# It exists as a script rather than two ExecStart lines so that an expired
# registry token explains itself. The image is private, so the pull needs a
# credential, and a credential that expires is the one predictable way this
# deploy breaks months from now - at which point nobody will remember any of
# this. So the failure prints its own fix.
#
# Managed by the docker-dagger repo; do not edit on the host.

cd /home/media

log=$(mktemp)
trap 'rm -f "$log"' EXIT

if ! docker compose pull td-server 2>&1 | tee "$log"; then
  if grep -qi 'unauthorized\|denied\|authentication required' "$log"; then
    cat >&2 <<'INSTRUCTIONS'

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The registry rejected us. The TrageD image is private, so this is almost
certainly the read:packages token having expired or been revoked.

To rotate it:

  1. Mint a classic PAT with ONLY the `read:packages` scope:
       https://github.com/settings/tokens/new?scopes=read:packages
     Give it a long expiry and a name like "dagger ghcr pull".

  2. Install it on this host (reads the token from stdin, writes it
     root-only to /etc/traged/docker/config.json):

       cd /home/media && printf '%s' '<TOKEN>' | sudo ./traged/set-registry-token

  3. Update the 1Password item "TrageD GHCR pull token (dagger)" in the
     Private vault, including the new expiry date in its notes.

  4. Re-run the deploy:  gh workflow run deploy   (from a td checkout)

Nothing else is wrong: the container currently running is untouched, and
the site on sabre is unaffected.
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

INSTRUCTIONS
  fi
  exit 1
fi

# --no-deps so a game deploy never restarts Plex or anything else the house is
# using; only the one service is replaced.
#
# --wait blocks until the container reports healthy rather than returning the
# moment it is started. Without it the deploy claims success while the container
# is still booting, and anything checking immediately afterwards sees a 404:
# Traefik's docker provider drops the router entirely while the container is
# away, so the hostname matches nothing at all rather than matching a backend
# that is down.
exec docker compose up -d --no-deps --wait --wait-timeout 90 td-server
